/K [4]
>>
endobj
203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The 2011 intervention in Libya stands as a watershed moment in the modern discourse on humanitarian intervention and the practical application of the Responsibility to Protect \(R2P\) doctrine. The context was the rapid escalation of violence following the outbreak of protests against the government, which were met with significant force, raising fears of mass atrocities.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Initially, the implementation focused on preventing aerial attacks against civilian populations. However, the scope of operations gradually expanded as international forces engaged ground targets and supported opposition groups. What began as a narrowly defined civilian protection mandate soon evolved into actions that effectively facilitated regime change.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The collapse of central authority and the absence of a coordinated post-conflict stabilization plan left Libya in a prolonged state of fragmentation, marked by competing governments, regional militias, and a persistent lack of security. This case highlights several critical issues in humanitarian intervention. First, once military operations commence, maintaining a strict separation between humanitarian objectives and political or strategic outcomes becomes increasingly difficult. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 123 0 R
/K [1]
/Pg 11 0 R
>>
endobj
208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (GDGPSMUN)
/E (GDGPSMUN)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (GDGPSMUN)
/E (GDGPSMUN)
/Pg 11 0 R
