>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 124 0 R
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
213 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Second, the presence of armed opposition groups embedded within civilian populations complicated the task of distinguishing legitimate protection efforts from active participation in a civil conflict. Third, the destruction of core state institutions without adequate investment in transitional governance and reconstruction generated long-term instability, weakening possibilities for sustainable peace.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
214 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Today, Libya illustrates the consequences of intervention without comprehensive post-conflict planning. Large parts of the country remain outside the control of recognized national authorities, while various armed actors, some with external backing, compete for territorial and political dominance. The United Nations Support Mission in Libya \(UNSMIL\), established in 2011, continues to struggle with facilitating dialogue between numerous non-state armed actors while supporting civilian protection.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
215 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Syria: The Limits of Intervention)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The ongoing conflict in Syria provides a clear illustration of the significant constraints on humanitarian intervention when consensus among major powers cannot be reached. Despite substantial evidence of mass atrocities, including systematic attacks on healthcare facilities in direct violation of Security Council Resolution 2286 \(2016\), the Council was unable to take decisive action due to the Russian and Chinese Vetoes.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This paralysis highlights the structural challenges within the international system when urgent protection needs arise in situations of high geopolitical contention. Within Syria, armed non-state actors operate across a broad spectrum. On one end are relatively moderate opposition groups seeking to contest state authority, while on the other are proscribed organizations designated as terrorist entities under international law. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
218 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 124 0 R
/K [1]
/Pg 12 0 R
>>
endobj
