264 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Children and Armed Conflict)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
265 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The recruitment and use of children by armed groups remains one of the most pressing protection challenges in contemporary conflicts. Children are exploited in a variety of roles, ranging from combatants and porters to intelligence gatherers and, in some contexts, forced marriage or sexual slavery. Such practices constitute grave violations of international humanitarian and human rights law, as reflected in the Optional Protocol to the Convention on the Rights of the Child on the involvement of children in armed conflict \(2000\) and reinforced by the Paris Principles \(2007\).)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
266 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The United Nations has consistently highlighted this issue through the Children and Armed Conflict \(CAAC\) agenda, which identifies six grave violations against children in conflict situations, including recruitment, killing and maiming, and attacks on schools and hospitals. In this regard, Security Council Resolution 2764 \(2024\) highlights the importance of strengthening child protection capacities within peace operations and during transitions, ensuring that child-specific expertise is integrated into mandates, reporting mechanisms, and disarmament, demobilization, and reintegration \(DDR\) programs.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
